edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115374 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c6f8a463-c804-5c12-af05-532e56a257e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186546988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115375 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c554a0f7-dfa1-5329-b149-60e64aa1faab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling Mixture Problems With First-Order Separable ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115463 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b958274c-e6c0-599a-87d9-822a2901b003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1071 "A $9\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity tank initially holds $6\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $180\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3617a1e8-dceb-5727-a7bd-10880de46d8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115793 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $6\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed brine leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,000+90t-60t=30(200+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{200+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{200+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=180,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =180 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{2}} & =180 \\\\ A & =180(200)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{180(200)^{2}}{(200+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =180{(\\frac{200}{200+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $9\\,000\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,000$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,000 \\\\ 30(200+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =180{(\\frac{200}{200+100})}^{2} \\\\  & =180{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1071 "A $9\\,800\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity tank initially holds $7\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $196\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $105\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $70\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a53b4e44-7f34-5742-ba72-ac1886cc69b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115796 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $105\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (105\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (105\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $105\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed brine leaves at $70\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,000+105t-70t=35(200+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $70\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(70\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(70\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{200+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{200+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $196\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=196,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =196 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{2}} & =196 \\\\ A & =196(200)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{196(200)^{2}}{(200+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =196{(\\frac{200}{200+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $9\\,800\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,800$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,800 \\\\ 35(200+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,800 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =280 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =80\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(80) & =196{(\\frac{200}{200+80})}^{2} \\\\  & =196{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1061 593289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca6cb0e8-4c90-5c6d-bf08-803296752852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1061 593290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d55c798d-8387-51fe-9d00-6c58dd6d6dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116337 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1061 593291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "251dbebe-a8e1-57f6-b8c1-54c43e9332c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116338 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1061 593294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28ce70f4-759b-530e-a692-0d92aa4ffb8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116339 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1061 593295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d7e1fc1-8a54-5f2f-85f5-542931de3855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116340 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1061 593299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "259727bc-a70f-53ae-9206-f6024bda6e30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116341 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116342 #edb/ref 1061 593300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116342 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5981b624-3f7f-52e8-a1c9-8c3bd471d2b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116342 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time.\nThe rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},\n$$\n\n where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\n So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft})\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}\n$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1065 14562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be8f798d-68e4-549b-a2c6-ce3fd435c044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116425 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant.\nA solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nrepresents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1065 14563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b84bba1-b479-506a-8e25-77650b293eee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116426 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1064 "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.\n$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, \n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0.\n$$\n\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1065 14564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3c551e08-3014-52f3-83f1-73ec27aa2e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116427 #edb/ref 1067 "Points of Overflow and Emptying" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have seen that if a container initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance, and\n\n- a solution with concentration $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ flows into the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ while\n\n- the completely mixed solution flows out at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min},$\n\nthen the amount $Q(t)$ of the substance in the container satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = be.\n$$\n\nFor the rest of this lesson, we'll focus on the special case where the input solution contains *no substance*, that is, where $b=0.$ In this case, the right-hand side vanishes, and the differential equation becomes *separable*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac1Q\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = - \\dfrac{f}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is negative, meaning the amount of substance in the container decreases over time. We can now solve the differential equation directly by separation of variables.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider our previous example: a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, and a solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nWe found that the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank after $t$ minutes is modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{4}{225}Q = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{4}{225}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by integrating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}\\,dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{225})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{4}{225}\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{4t}{225}+C \\\\ Q & ={e}^{-4t/225+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-4t/225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nFinally, since the tank initially contains $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-4(0)/225} & =6 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{Q(t) = 6e^{-4t/225}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1065 14654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a4e43cc-21fe-5b6e-ab72-10aef2431693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116428 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving a Dilution Model Where the Input Has a Concentration of Zero" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374774 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "25d457fb-82ff-5f69-8b22-551ddc28ab54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374775 #edb/ref 1080 "100\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374775 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374775 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c976b7a-3ff5-5927-ba0d-b085f9146139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374776 #edb/ref 1080 "85\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374776 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374776 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fdd80501-68fe-5cf1-b205-347bb8c82c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374777 #edb/ref 1080 "80\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374777 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374777 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e3ce3cd-cbeb-5761-acd5-4e01b6f728eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374778 #edb/ref 1080 "90\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374778 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374778 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad0d45c5-18ff-5a0b-a685-60dfd26d7a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374779 #edb/ref 1080 "82\\,\\text{g}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374779 #edb/ref 1082